Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Postcards Rust Along the Seawall
- Story & Context: In the catastrophic cyclone of July 1987, pirate broadcaster Kavi Patel lashed his medium-wave transmitter to the fish-drying racks of Porbandar harbor, only discovering that the gear sparked to life when lightning ionized the antenna wire. Inside his salt-encrusted wooden cabin, smelling of wet kerosene lamps and ozone, Patel sent an SOS via a 432Hz synth drone overlaid with an explosive, gated LinnDrum clap that stunned radio operators across the Arabian Sea. The broadcast preserved the distress calls of coastal postmen whose mailbags had been swept into the sea, turning emergency telemetry into a devotional rain raga. His raspy whisper through the carbon microphone became the scene's first gospel: "the sea delivers what the postmaster forgot."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Album cover art in 1980s saturated film style showing a pile of handwritten inland letters half-submerged in wet sea sand on a stormy beach, their ink bleeding indigo into the foam while a distant lighthouse flashes against thunderclouds.

Track 2: "Did the Registered Dispatch Wash Ashore?"
- Story & Context: Inside the flooded telegraph exchange of Veraval, night clerk Sunita Bose spent three monsoon seasons safeguarding hundreds of undelivered official parcels whose wax seals had melted in the damp heat. During an August squall, as lightning repeatedly activated Radio Meghdoot's illicit frequency on her switchboard monitor, Sunita plugged her microphone into the transmitter and confessed live to burning her estranged fiancé's final overseas letter. Her sorrowful vocal glides drifted across floating Yamaha DX7 bells tuned to Raga Darbari, her confession punctuating each thunderous digital handclap as the water lapped against her ankles. Before pulling the circuit breaker, she spoke the question that haunted maritime listeners for years: "I kept your stamp uncancelled while the storm took the harbor."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A lyric video still showing a vintage 1980s telegraph desk underwater under flickering fluorescent light, a brass telegraph key resting beside water-damaged blue postal paper with cursive ink melting into sea foam.

Track 3: Dak-Taar Megh
- Story & Context: Long after Radio Meghdoot's physical antenna was snapped in half by the 1989 typhoon, trawler crews navigating the Gulf of Kutch reported that tuning their dashboard cassette-radios to 432 kHz during squalls still pulled down a phantom broadcast. This final track was allegedly reconstructed from a waterlogged magnetic tape found inside an uncollected postal locker in Okha port. The composition consists entirely of a glacial, microtonal Megh raga drone anchored by a deafening, tape-saturated clap that sounds like a heavy brass postal stamp striking an empty desk. Coastal mythology claims that if the clap coincides with a flash of cloud-to-ground lightning, you can hear dead postmasters singing the chorus through the squall: "the storm has stamped your envelope with water."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A grainy, surrealist 1980s airbrushed illustration of a giant brass postal rubber stamp falling from a dark cumulonimbus cloud onto a stormy ocean, glowing electric blue arcs jumping between the stamp and the churning waves.

